    doesn't exist, so nothing works end-to-end until a backend does; needs error/network handling now.
  - [1mAsync signature, stubbed body[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is [38;5;153masync[39m and returns a synthesized userId without a network call, with the
    [38;5;153mfetch[39m dropped in later. Buys: the contagious part of the decision is paid once, now, while there's exactly one
    caller; swapping the body later touches only [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. Costs: slightly odd-looking async function with no await in it
    today.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third.[0m The expensive, repo-wide part of this choice is the async signature, not the fetch;
  making it async now while there's a single caller costs one line, and doing it after you've added the other forms
  costs all of them.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should login() produce the userId, given the API is a stub? → Async signature, stub body (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last clarifying question before I present the design — what the session module actually needs to do. I want to YAGNI
  this hard rather than build a speculative auth layer, so I'd rather you tell me than guess.

  Minimum to satisfy what you've described is store-on-login plus read-from-anywhere. Everything beyond that is real
  work and real edge cases: a clear/logout path needs a UI affordance to trigger it, expiry needs a timestamp and a
  staleness rule on every read, and cross-tab sync needs a [38;5;153mstorage[39m event listener. Each is cheap to add later on top of
  a clean module — none of them changes the interface the other forms use, which is why I'm comfortable leaving them out
  now.

